WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.849
Correct policy logic can still fail open
through control flow.

2
00:00:03.929 --> 00:00:07.467
Express-style middleware may call next
after reporting a denial,

3
00:00:07.547 --> 00:00:11.464
an early response may lack return, a
promise may be started without await,

4
00:00:11.544 --> 00:00:14.324
or an error handler may resume a route
unexpectedly.

5
00:00:14.404 --> 00:00:18.827
Structure the guard so every non-allow
branch returns or throws and the only path to

6
00:00:18.907 --> 00:00:20.676
next is after a recognized allow.

7
00:00:20.756 --> 00:00:24.926
Avoid mixed callback and promise
conventions that permit double completion.

8
00:00:25.006 --> 00:00:28.986
Missing await is especially dangerous
because a Promise object is truthy.

9
00:00:29.066 --> 00:00:33.552
Code that tests an unresolved
authorization promise can enter the protected branch

10
00:00:33.632 --> 00:00:35.148
before any decision exists.

11
00:00:35.228 --> 00:00:39.714
Enable lint rules for floating promises
and make authorization APIs hard to misuse,

12
00:00:39.794 --> 00:00:42.637
but retain runtime tests around the actual
call path.

13
00:00:42.717 --> 00:00:45.624
A compiler configuration or linter reduces
mistakes;

14
00:00:45.704 --> 00:00:48.168
it does not replace the enforcement
boundary.

15
00:00:48.248 --> 00:00:52.670
Ensure rejected promises and late
callbacks cannot trigger a second continuation

16
00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:55.530
after the request has already entered an
error path.

17
00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:59.907
Do not rely exclusively on a global route
guard when authorization depends on the

18
00:00:59.987 --> 00:01:01.124
resource and action.

19
00:01:01.204 --> 00:01:04.426
Middleware can authenticate and establish
common context.

20
00:01:04.506 --> 00:01:09.118
Object-specific authorization often
belongs in or near the application service that

21
00:01:09.198 --> 00:01:12.547
has the trusted resource attributes and
knows the side effect.

22
00:01:12.627 --> 00:01:17.113
Sensitive services should require
AuthContext and an explicit command rather than

23
00:01:17.193 --> 00:01:19.088
accept an optional request object.

24
00:01:19.168 --> 00:01:23.085
Trace every entry point: REST routes,
GraphQL resolvers, queue workers,

25
00:01:23.165 --> 00:01:27.714
scheduled processes, internal RPC,
administrative tools, and background retries.

26
00:01:27.794 --> 00:01:30.827
A job may act with a service identity or
on behalf of a user,

27
00:01:30.907 --> 00:01:33.434
but that authority must be explicit and
scoped.

28
00:01:33.514 --> 00:01:37.747
Centralize reusable policy evaluation and
failure translation while keeping

29
00:01:37.827 --> 00:01:39.849
operation-specific checks visible.

30
00:01:39.929 --> 00:01:44.352
Service reuse is safe only when alternate
callers cannot bypass the authorization

31
00:01:44.432 --> 00:01:46.580
required by the underlying capability.
